  "summary": "A horizontal multistage pump places several impellers in series on one shaft. Each stage — impeller, diffuser and return channel — adds roughly the same head, so the pressure builds up stage by stage from suction to discharge. The impeller flings liquid outward and raises its velocity and pressure; the diffuser vanes slow it down and convert velocity into more pressure; the return vanes guide it back inward, without swirl, to the eye of the next impeller. Because every impeller faces the same way, their axial thrusts add up; a balance drum at the discharge end, with a leak-off line back to suction, cancels most of it, and a thrust bearing takes the rest. Multistage pumps serve high-head duties such as boiler feed, reverse-osmosis feed, high-rise water supply, mine dewatering and pressure boosting.",
  "classification": {
    "family": "Rotodynamic (centrifugal), radial flow, multistage",
    "configuration": "Horizontal, between bearings, radially split ring-section (segmental)",
    "api_610_type": "BB4 (single casing, radially split, multistage); barrel BB5 and axially split BB3 are alternatives"
  },
  "model_configuration": {
    "stages": 6,
    "impellers": "Closed, same hand, in series (inline arrangement)",
    "stage_hardware": "Stage casing + diffuser vanes + return (cross-over) vanes per stage, clamped by 8 tie bolts",
    "axial_thrust": "Balance drum + balance bush + leak-off line to suction; thrust bearing for the residual",
    "alternatives": "Balance disc, opposed (back-to-back) impellers, or thrust bearing only (small pumps)",
    "teaching_overlays": "pressure (stage-by-stage), thrust (impeller thrust vs balance-drum force), flow"
  },
